# DESCRIPTION
# A problem that asks the student to find
# the formula of a line from a plot..
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear equations and functions)
## DBsection(Graphs of lines)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## Level(1)
## KEYWORDS('graph, lines, linear functions')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl',
    'plots.pl',      'contextFraction.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Fraction");
Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y');

do {
    ($x0, $y0) = (random(-5, 5), random(-5, 5));
    ($x1, $y1) = (random(-5, 5), random(-5, 5));
} until $x0 != $x1;

$m = Fraction($y0 - $y1, $x0 - $x1);
$b = Compute($y0 - $x0 * $m);
$f = Formula("$m x + $b")->reduce;

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -6.5,
    xmax            =>  6.5,
    xtick_delta     =>  1,
    xminor          =>  0,
    ymin            => -6.5,
    ymax            =>  6.5,
    ytick_delta     =>  1,
    yminor          =>  0,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function("$f", 'x', -6.5, 6.5, color => 'darkblue');

$alttext = "graph of a line through ($x0,$y0) and ($x1,$y1).";
BEGIN_PGML
Consider the graph

>>[! [$alttext] !]{$plot}{300} <<

What is the formula of this line?
[`y=`] [_]{$f}{35}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

In this case, we need to find the equation of the line based on the graph.
It's generally easiest to find the equation in slope-intercept form, so we
will need both the slope and the [`y`]-intercept.  It hard to just look at the
line to get either the slope or the [`y`]-intercept, but it appears that there
are two points that are easy to see on the graph (since they fall on integer
coordinates).

These points appear to be [` ([$x0],[$y0])`] and [` ([$x1],[$y1])`], so we will
find the line that passes through these points.  First, find the slope from
the two points:

[`` m = \frac{[$y1]-[$y0]}{[$x1]-[$x0]} = [$m] ``]

Then substitute this into the slope-intercept form of the line:

[`` y = [$m] x + b ``]

Next, substitute in the point [` ([$x0],[$y0])`] or

[``
\begin{aligned}
[$y0] & = ([$m])([$x0]) + b \\
[$y0] & = [$m*$x0] + b
\end{aligned}
``]

Then, [@ $m*$x0 > 0 ? 'subtract' : 'add' @]  [` [@abs($m*$x0)@] `] [@ $m*$x0 > 0 ? 'from' : 'to' @] both sides:

[`` [$b] = b``]
 and finally plug this value of b into the equation:

[`` y = [$f] ``]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
